    "post": "\"Apple copied Braun\"\n\nEvery time I hear that… I kinda just think:\n\n“Yeh, that’s how the creative industry works”\n\nPeople tend to miss the bigger lesson here.\nGood design has always been remix culture.\nIf you stare at enough products for long enough, you realize:\n\nNobody creates in a vacuum.\n\nDesigners study:\n◦ patterns\n◦ principles\n◦ visual language\n◦ human behaviour\n◦ interaction feedback\n◦ systems that already work\n◦ things users already understand\n\nThen they refine it.\n\nApple didn’t become Apple by copying a calculator layout.\n\nThey understood why those Braun products felt so clean, calm, and functional. Then they applied the same principles consistently across hardware, software, packaging, retail, marketing.\n\nDoing that at Apple’s scale is ridiculously hard.\n\nMost designers are less original than they think. TBH, that’s not a bad thing.\n\nGreat creative work comes from people paying super close attention for a very long time.\n\nHalf of Pinterest probably exists because of “yo this is sick… let me study that”\n\nSo, is Apple just knock-off of Braun?\n\nOr one hell of a remix?\n\nP.S. Have you ever saved a design for “inspiration” and accidentally recreated 70% of it later?\n\nP.P.S. More creative rants—live, distilled, and in your inbox: rectangles.fm\n\n📌 Help me make rectangles design’s group chat (takes 5 minutes): rectangles.fm/survey",
